v3d: block the binner only when a vertex texture may still be being written

The binner runs the coordinate shader before the job's render, and the kernel orders a bin job only
by its explicit in_sync, so a vertex shader that samples a texture a still running GPU job writes
must wait for it. v3d made every bin job of a draw with any vertex texture wait for the context's
last render ("rather than tracking the last rendering to each texture's BO"). Godot's vertex shaders
(Waze's map) sample textures that no GPU job writes, so every bin job of the frame waited for the
render before it, and V3D's render engine idled while each bin ran (Pi 4, Waze at 1184x928: bin jobs
waited 3.5 s per second for renders; the render engine was busy 81% of the time).

Each resource now remembers the context and the submit sequence number of the last render or TFU
job that writes it (cbufs, zsbuf and its separate stencil, the TLB blit destination, the job's
write set and transform feedback targets, the TFU's destination), and the context remembers up to
which sequence number every job had completed when a bin job that waited on out_sync was submitted
(bin jobs run in submission order). A draw's bin job waits when a sampled vertex texture (the
view's resource, or the shadow copy the TMU samples) was last written by a job of this context
after that point, or by another context (as before: wait on our own last render). Indirect drawing
still always waits; compute writes keep sync_on_last_compute_job.

Measured on a Pi 4 (stock clocks), Waze's car map at 1184x928: bin jobs no longer wait (mean wait
31.8 ms -> 0.7 ms), the render engine busy 81% -> 91%, 26.1/26.5 -> 26.8/27.4 frames/s.
tools/debian/v3d-mesa-test.c checks it against the GL's results (a vertex shader reading what a
slow draw, a clear and glGenerateMipmap's TFU just wrote, and a CPU upload; a build without the
wait fails 272 of 900 checks, this one passes all).

Applies to Mesa 26.3.1 (tools/debvm/versions.env MESA_*), patch -p1 in the source tree.

